  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3k}{6+4x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3k}{6+4y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3k}{6+4y} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3k}{6+4y} \\\\ x(6+4y) & =3k \\\\ 6x+4xy & =3k \\\\ 4xy & =3k-6x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3k-6x}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3k-6x}{4x}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=3.$",
